---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import BenchChart from "../components/BenchChart.astro";
import BenchMatrix from "../components/BenchMatrix.astro";
import BrowserBench from "../playground/BrowserBench";
import { BUNDLE, WRITE, formatMb } from "../data/bench";
import { GITHUB } from "../data/site";
---

<Base
  title="@kuramajs/excel benchmarks: speed and memory vs SheetJS and exceljs"
  description="Time, peak memory and bundle size when reading 1M-row Excel files with @kuramajs/excel, SheetJS and exceljs. Reproduce it, or run it on your own file."
>
  <div class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">Benchmarks</div>
      <h1>Faster, and a fraction of the memory.</h1>
      <p>
        Parse a sheet and read every row. Each case runs in a fresh Node process (Node 22, Apple Silicon, median of 3 runs,
        peak RSS including Node's ~43 MB baseline). Generated files with 8 columns of mixed types.
        <a href={`${GITHUB}/tree/master/bench`} style="color:var(--green)">Methodology and scripts →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap bench-page">
    <BenchChart />

    <BenchMatrix />

    <h2 id="write">Writing</h2>
    <p class="sub">Build the same 8-column rows and produce the finished xlsx bytes. Same setup as above.</p>
    <div class="bundle">
      <table class="data">
        <thead><tr><th>Write xlsx</th><th class="r">100k rows</th><th class="r">1M rows</th><th class="r">Peak memory (1M)</th><th class="r">File (1M)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {WRITE.map((w) => (
            <tr class:list={{ us: w.ours }}>
              <td>{w.lib}</td>
              <td class="r">{w.s[0]} s</td>
              <td class="r">{w.s[1] === null ? "out of memory" : `${w.s[1]} s`}</td>
              <td class="r">{w.mb[1] === null ? "—" : formatMb(w.mb[1])}</td>
              <td class="r">{w.file === null ? "—" : `${w.file} MB`}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Honest note: exceljs's streaming writer is faster at 1M rows, but uses twice the memory. At 100k rows the two tie.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bundle">
      <table class="data">
        <thead><tr><th>Browser bundle</th><th class="r">Raw</th><th class="r">Gzip</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {BUNDLE.map((b) => <tr class:list={{ us: b.ours }}><td>{b.lib}</td><td class="r">{b.raw} KB</td><td class="r">{b.gzip} KB</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Honest note: the reader is the largest download gzipped. The writer is a separate import, so apps that only read never load it. Most of it is the parsers for five formats plus text-encoding tables that xlsx and ods also need.
        Load it lazily, or in a worker, so it never blocks your first paint.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="run">Run it in your browser</h2>
    <p class="sub">Drop any spreadsheet. Each library parses it in a Web Worker on your machine. Nothing is uploaded.</p>
    <BrowserBench client:only="react" />
  </div>
</Base>
